{0 Bindings}

A binding connects your Worker to a Cloudflare resource, such as a key-value
namespace or a database. You declare it in [wrangler.toml]. At run time it is a
name in [env], and you open it with [of_env].

{1 What workero supports}

- {{!page-kv}KV}: {!Workero.KV}, Workers KV namespaces.
- {{!page-cache}Cache}: {!Workero.Cache}, the Cache API.
- {{!page-r2}R2}: {!Workero.R2}, object storage.
- {{!page-d1}D1}: {!Workero.D1} and {!Workero.Sql_value}, SQL databases.
- {{!page-fetcher}Fetcher}: {!Workero.Fetcher}, service bindings and static
  assets.
- {{!page-queue}Queue}: {!Workero.Queue}, producers and consumers of Cloudflare
  Queues.
- {{!page-rate_limiter}Rate limiting}: {!Workero.Rate_limiter}.
- {{!page-hyperdrive}Hyperdrive}: {!Workero.Hyperdrive}, connection details for
  a database (no query driver yet).
- {{!page-ai}Workers AI}: {!Workero.Ai}.
- {{!page-analytics_engine}Analytics Engine}: {!Workero.Analytics_engine}.
- {{!page-env}Secrets Store}: {!Workero.Secret_store}, secrets that Workers
  share.

String variables and secrets are not bindings. {!Workero.Env.var} and
{!Workero.Env.secret} read them. See {{!page-env}Variables and secrets}.

{1 Declare a binding}

Each resource has a table in [wrangler.toml]. [binding] is the name that you
pass to [of_env]:

{@toml[
[[kv_namespaces]]
binding = "KV"
id = "<namespace id>"

[[r2_buckets]]
binding = "BUCKET"
bucket_name = "my-bucket"

[[d1_databases]]
binding = "DB"
database_name = "my-database"
database_id = "<database id>"

[[services]]
binding = "BACKEND"
service = "backend-worker"

[[queues.producers]]
binding = "JOBS"
queue = "jobs"
]}

The Cache API has no binding. {!Workero.Cache} uses the cache of the data
center that runs the Worker.

{1 Use a binding}

[of_env env name] returns the binding. It raises [Invalid_argument] when [env]
has no binding with that name. The message names it. Open the binding inside the
handler, where you have [env].

Every operation suspends like {!Workero.Promise.await}, so you call it in direct
style. When the runtime fails, for example on an SQL error or a network error,
the operation raises {!Workero.Js_error}. The payload is a
{!Workero.Promise.error}, the original JavaScript error. That includes errors
that JavaScript throws before it returns a Promise.
{!Workero.Promise.error_to_string} gives ["Name: message"].

{1 Develop locally}

[wrangler dev] runs bindings against local storage by default. KV, R2 and D1
need no Cloudflare account for this, and the data stays on your machine. Create
the resources for a deploy with Wrangler:

{@sh[
npx wrangler kv namespace create KV
npx wrangler r2 bucket create my-bucket
npx wrangler d1 create my-database
]}

Each command prints the values for [wrangler.toml]. Each binding page has a
complete Worker.
